<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="pheomelanin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Description>|FRAME: CPD-12380 Pheomelanin| is a common type of melanin pigment, which is found in hair and skin. Pheomelanin imparts a pink to red hue and is accordingly found in large quantities in red hair. In skin pheomelanin is particularly concentrated in the lips, nipples, glans of the penis, and vagina. Chemically, pheomelanin consists mostly of benzothiazine units, which are degraded to benzothiazole units to some extent |CITS: [4210254]|. It is difficult to specify the exact structure of melanin polymers, since they are composed of many different units that are connected through strong carbon-carbon bonds. The pathway starts with tyrosinase (|FRAME: HS01248|), which catalyzes the initial, rate-limiting step of melanogenesis, forming |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE|. This compound is extremely reactive, and in the presence of |FRAME: CYS| reacts spontaneously to form |FRAME: CPD-20260| and |FRAME: CPD-15154| |CITS: [61113][408183]|. These reactions are rapid, and continue as long as cysteine is present |CITS: [10952395]|. In an experiment conducted with a tyrosinae from the mushroom |FRAME: TAX-5341| the ratio beween |FRAME: CPD-15154| and |FRAME: CPD-20260| was 5:1 |CITS: [408183]|. It should be noted that in the absence of |FRAME: CYS|, |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE| undergoes several spontaneous rearrangements, generating |FRAME: CPD-8652| and |FRAME: L-DOPACHROME|, leading to production of |FRAME: CPD-12379| (see |FRAME: PWY-6498|). Thus, the presence of |FRAME: CYS| is a key determinant for the production of either |FRAME: CPD-12379| or |FRAME: CPD-12380| |CITS: [9367182]|. Once the |FRAME: CYS| concentration decreases to 30 times lower than the |FRAME: CPD-15154| concentration, another reaction becomes predominant - the oxidation of the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-cysteinyldopa compounds by |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE| generating their quinone forms (|FRAME: CPD-20261| and |FRAME: CPD-20262|, respectively). |FRAME: DOPAQUINONE Dopaquinone| is reduced to |FRAME: L-DIHYDROXY-PHENYLALANINE| |CITS: [10952395][12753395]|. Once the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-cysteinyldopaquinones are formed, they rapidly cyclize (via an attack of the cysteinyl side-chain amino group on the carbonyl group) to give cyclic &lt;i&gt;o&lt;/i&gt;-quinonimine intermediates that tautomerize to a benzothiazine intermediates, which then react to form |FRAME: CPD-12380| |CITS: [11744399]|.</Description>
